# Application managers.
#
# How software gets ONTO an image whose rootfs is read-only: everything
# here installs into the user's home or /var/lib/flatpak, both of which
# live on the data partition. Included by the desktop frontends
# (frontend/gnome.config, frontend/xfce.config) - a game-frontend
# handheld gets its content through EmulationStation and PortMaster
# instead.
#
# Not a store monoculture, deliberately:
#
#   flatpak + GNOME Software   general applications, from Flathub et al.
#   lutris                     games from installer scripts, GOG, itch.io,
#                              plus the Wine/emulator plumbing they need
#   heroic                     GOG / Epic / Amazon libraries (x86_64 only:
#                              upstream publishes no Linux arm64 build,
#                              and it is the tree's one prebuilt package -
#                              Electron is not buildable here)
#
# All of them follow the image's content/state split (see S15data):
#
#   content (reinstallable)      state (the user's)
#   /data/roms/flatpak           /data/saves/flatpak   (~/.var)
#   /data/roms/lutris            /data/saves/lutris    (data + config)
#   /data/roms/heroic            /data/saves/heroic    (config + tools)
#   /data/roms/ports             /data/saves/<port>    (via es-launch)
#   /data/roms/steam             /data/saves/steam     (client + library vdf)
#
# via home-skeleton symlinks for state and seeded default install paths
# for content. Everything survives reboots and A/B updates, and each side
# can be backed up without the other.
#
# The steam row: the client and everything it writes live in the save
# tree (~/.local/share/Steam is a symlink there), and steam-run registers
# /roms/steam as a game library, so installs can land with the rest of
# the content. Steam itself is declared in emulation.config - it ships on
# every 64-bit image, not just the desktops - and appears among the
# frontend's seeded entries via the Steam system (see es_systems.cfg).
#
# The ports row is PortMaster's: it expands into and installs under
# /roms/ports (the /data/roms/ports it resolves to), and a port launched
# from the frontend gets XDG_DATA_HOME pointed into the save tree by
# es-launch. The one deviation is PortMaster's own directory,
# /roms/ports/PortMaster - it self-updates and keeps its settings in
# place, so manager and state ride together there by upstream design.

BR2_PACKAGE_FLATPAK=y
BR2_PACKAGE_GNOME_SOFTWARE=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LUTRIS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_HEROIC=y

# PortMaster - the ports row of the table above. The frontend fragment
# declares it as well (handheld images do not include this fragment);
# here it reaches the ARM desktop images, where the frontend they build
# gives it its Tools entry. Its Config.in gates it to ARM with
# EmulationStation, so on x86_64 desktops the line is inert.
BR2_PACKAGE_PORTMASTER=y

# Steam
BR2_PACKAGE_STEAM=y
